  "renderRecommendation": {
    "hexTerrainArtPxMultiplier": {
      "current": 1.1,
      "recommended": 1.8,
      "rationale": "The traced glyphs already fill 55-74% of their own 100x100 box \u2014 comparable to the original multicoloured art's own 45-94% fill ratio \u2014 so the shapes are not undersized; the render-time box (size*1.1) is. At 1.1x a terrain glyph and a feature/contents icon (drawn at the same centre, per renderHexTiles) nearly perfectly overlap and the terrain reads as barely more than a colour. At 1.8x (clipped to the hex boundary) a wide/sprawling glyph (forest canopy, mountain double-peak, hills' arches) clearly separates from a centred feature icon in both true size (48px) and study size. One residual case: a compact, narrow glyph (swamp's drip cluster) under a large filled-circle icon (the \"town\" contents icon) still mostly disappears even at 1.8x, because the glyph's own silhouette does not extend far enough from centre for scaling to help \u2014 colour alone still carries the terrain there. 2.2x was also studied and reads almost identically to 1.8x while clipping more aggressively; 1.8x is the recommended value."
    }
  },
  "pickerRecommendation": {
    "retireFromNewAuthoringPalette": [
      "palm",
      "plateau",
      "grass",
      "scrub",
      "tundra",
      "ice-floe"
    ],
    "rationale": "Confirmed with the user (2026-09-09): these six kinds now draw identical art to another kind in the palette (palm=jungle, plateau=hills, grass=grassland, scrub=cactus, tundra=ice-floe=snowfields) \u2014 a picker offering both reads as a confusing duplicate, not backward compatibility. They stay in HEX_TERRAIN_CATALOG and keep resolving (RULE-007: alias, never rename) for any hex a referee already painted with the old kind; WI-120 should simply not surface them in whatever UI lets a referee choose a kind to paint a *new* hex."
  },
